.TH CONTROL 2
.SH NAME
control: tf, series, feedback, roots, routh, stable, stepresponse, bode, margins, metrics, pid \- linear systems as transfer functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "control.m";
control := load Control Control->PATH;
Tf, Margins, Metrics: import control;

control->init();
(g, err) := control->tf(num, den);
(cl, err) = control->feedback(g, h, -1);
(y, err) := control->stepresponse(cl, dt, n);
m := control->metrics(y, dt);
(mag, ph) := control->bode(g, w);
(mm, err) := control->margins(g);
(n, err) := control->routh(den);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Control
is linear systems written the way a controls course writes them: a
transfer function, a ratio of polynomials in
.IR s .
.PP
Coefficients are given
.B highest
.B power
.BR first ,
the way they are written down, so
.B "{1.0, 3.0}"
over
.B "{1.0, 2.0, 5.0}"
is
.RI ( s +3)/( s \u\s-22\s+2\d+2\fIs\fP+5).
The other order exists in other libraries and nothing in an answer
reveals which was meant.
.PP
Everything is continuous-time.
A discrete system is a different object with a different stability test,
and mixing the two silently is the classic way to get a confident wrong
answer;
.IR signal (2)
is where the discrete filters live.
.SS Building and connecting
.B Tf
builds a transfer function, trimming leading zeros and refusing a
numerator of higher degree than the denominator \- a system that answers
before it is asked.
.B Series,
.B parallel
and
.B feedback
put two together;
.B feedback
with sign \-1 is the negative feedback every control loop is made of,
.IR g /(1+\fIgh\fP).
.B Polymul,
.B polyadd,
.B polyeval
and
.B polyfromroots
are the polynomial arithmetic underneath, exported because a caller
building a system by hand needs them.
.SS Roots and stability
.B Roots
finds every root of a polynomial at once, by Durand-Kerner, rather than
one at a time with deflation \- deflation bakes each root's error into
the polynomial the next is found from, and the last roots of a
high-order polynomial come out visibly wrong.
A real polynomial's real roots are returned with an imaginary part of
exactly zero, not the 1e-45 the iteration leaves: a plot would otherwise
show a conjugate pair where there is one root.
.B Poles
and
.B zeros
are the roots of the denominator and the numerator.
.PP
Stability is asked
.B twice
and on purpose.
.B Routh
answers it from the coefficients alone, by the Routh array, without
finding a single root, and returns the
.I number
of roots with positive real part.
.B Stable
answers it by finding the roots and looking at them.
They are independent routes to one fact, which is what makes either
believable;
.IR controltest (1)
runs them against each other on three thousand polynomials.
.SS Responses
.B Stepresponse,
.B impulseresponse
and
.B lsim
integrate the system in controllable canonical form with fourth-order
Runge-Kutta, ten sub-steps to a sample, so that how finely the answer is
sampled and how accurately it is integrated are separate questions.
An input to
.B lsim
is held constant between samples, as a computer driving a plant holds
it.
.PP
.B Bode
gives magnitude and phase at each frequency in radians per second.
The phase is unwrapped, so a high-order roll-off does not jump by 2 pi
in the middle.
.PP
.B Margins
gives the gain and phase margins with the frequencies they occur at.
The gain margin is a ratio and not decibels.
.B Hasgain
is zero when the phase never reaches \-180 degrees, which is
.I not
the same as a large margin and is why there is a flag rather than a big
number.
.PP
.B Metrics
reduces a step response to what a course asks about it: final value,
10-to-90 rise time, peak and the time of it, overshoot as a fraction of
the final value, and the last time the response was outside a 2% band.
.PP
It also reports whether the response
.B settled
inside the record it was given, and that field says whether to believe
the others.
All of them are computed from the final value, and the final value is
the average of the last tenth \- so a run that stops early reports a
final value that is merely where the curve happened to be, and an
overshoot measured against it.
The error is not small: a loop sampled for two thirds of one oscillation
reports an overshoot of 0.037 where the answer is 0.139.
It is detected by comparing the last tenth with the tenth before it,
since testing the last sample against the final value would not catch it
\- the final value is wrong in exactly the same way.
.SS Controllers
.B Pid
builds
.IR kp " + " ki /\fIs\fP " + " kd * s /(\fItaud\fP*\fIs\fP+1).
The derivative term needs that filter: a bare
.IR kd * s
is not proper, cannot be built, and differentiates the noise in
preference to the signal.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/control.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR circuit (2),
.IR signal (2),
.IR dynsys (2),
.IR controltest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All of these return a diagnostic string, nil on success.
.B Impulseresponse
refuses a system whose numerator degree equals its denominator's:
its impulse response contains a delta at
.I t
= 0 that no array of samples can hold, and returning the rest without
saying so would be a plot with a piece missing.
.SH BUGS
Single-input, single-output only.
There is no state-space interface, no MIMO, no observer or regulator
design, and no discrete-time transfer function.
.PP
.B Margins
sweeps a fixed grid of twenty thousand frequencies over eight decades
around the largest pole and interpolates between neighbours, so a system
with two crossings very close together will have only the first
reported.
.PP
There is no root locus.
It would be a sweep of
.B roots
over gain, which the pieces here support, but the plotting is the
substance of it and belongs with the plotting.
